## Solution

We compute $f(n) = \dfrac{1}{2^n}\sum_{i=0}^{(n+1)/2}(n-2i)\binom{n}{i}$ directly for each odd value.

**$n=1$:** Upper limit $(1+1)/2 = 1$.

$$\sum_{i=0}^{1}(1-2i)\binom{1}{i} = (1)(1) + (-1)(1) = 0, \quad f(1)=\frac{0}{2}=0.$$

**$n=3$:** Upper limit $(3+1)/2 = 2$.

$$\sum_{i=0}^{2}(3-2i)\binom{3}{i} = (3)(1)+(1)(3)+(-1)(3)=3+3-3=3, \quad f(3)=\frac{3}{8}.$$

**$n=5$:** Upper limit $(5+1)/2 = 3$.

$$\sum_{i=0}^{3}(5-2i)\binom{5}{i} = (5)(1)+(3)(5)+(1)(10)+(-1)(10)=5+15+10-10=20, \quad f(5)=\frac{20}{32}=\frac{5}{8}.$$

**$n=7$:** Upper limit $(7+1)/2 = 4$.

$$\sum_{i=0}^{4}(7-2i)\binom{7}{i} = (7)(1)+(5)(7)+(3)(21)+(1)(35)+(-1)(35)$$
$$= 7+35+63+35-35 = 105, \quad f(7)=\frac{105}{128}.$$

**Sum:**

$$f(1)+f(3)+f(5)+f(7) = 0 + \frac{3}{8} + \frac{5}{8} + \frac{105}{128} = \frac{8}{8} + \frac{105}{128} = 1 + \frac{105}{128} = \frac{233}{128}.$$

Since $233$ is prime and $128 = 2^7$, the fraction is already in lowest terms.

$$\boxed{\dfrac{233}{128}}$$

